course: dictionary
lesson: 14018
title: Magnification: bigger, or more important?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Magnification is a noun, made from the verb magnify, which means to make something look bigger. It has three meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. «The microscope has a magnification of ×40, so the tiny cell looks 40 times bigger.» Here it means how many times bigger something looks.
explain: «Through the magnifying glass, the ant looked enormous.» Here magnification is the making-bigger part: a lens or zoom does the work. When a word has more than one meaning, try each one in the sentence and see which fits best.
explain: Sometimes magnification means something different. «The newspaper's magnification of one small argument made it sound like a war.» Here the words make something seem more important or serious than it really is. The clue is the dramatic result: a small thing made to sound huge.
explain: In maths, magnification links to scale. A scale factor of 3 makes every length 3 times longer, just as a picture enlarged by 3 times is 3 times bigger. Phrases like «high magnification» and «under magnification» are clues that the science meaning is in use.
fact: Magnification is a noun, and its plural is magnifications.
fact: Magnification means making something look bigger than it really is, using a lens, microscope or zoom.
fact: In science, «×40» magnification means things look 40 times bigger.
fact: «Magnification» can also mean making something seem more important or serious than it is.
fact: In maths, a scale factor of 3 makes every length 3 times longer.
exercise: «The microscope has a magnification of ×40.» What does ×40 tell you? | Things are 40 years old | The microscope costs 40 pounds | Things weigh 40 grams | *Things look 40 times bigger
exercise: «The newspaper's magnification of one small argument made it sound like a war.» What does magnification mean here? | Shrinking something to make it smaller | Counting how many people were in the argument | *Making something seem more serious than it is | Making a small lens for a camera
exercise: In maths, a scale factor of 3 makes every length how big? | *3 times longer | 3 centimetres longer | 3 times shorter | The same size
test: «Through the magnifying glass, the ant looked enormous.» What is the magnifying glass doing to the ant? | Making it disappear | Painting it a new colour | Moving it far away | *Making it look bigger
test: «Under magnification, the leaf's veins were clear.» What does under magnification mean? | *Seen through a lens, so things look bigger | Far away from the sun | In a great hurry | Covered by a thin sheet
exam: Which sentence uses magnification in the sense of making something seem more serious? | «The microscope has a magnification of ×100.» | *«She turned one small scratch into a disaster, a real magnification of the problem.» | «The magnifying glass made the coin look huge.» | «The zoom on the camera has a high magnification.»
